  {
   "id": 672854442,
   "created_on": "2025-08-21T09:08:23.253704+00:00",
   "updated_on": "2025-08-21T09:08:55.320008+00:00",
   "content": {
    "type": "rendered",
    "raw": "This could have woeful performance due to:\n\n1. DB loading all of the client infos into memory from disk, sending across the network and loading into the application’s memory. Has the potential to OOM.\n2. Reaching out to the edge for potentially every client info if no match is found\n\nIt would be much more preferable to push the filtering down to the DB itself, where possible, and only query for a matching product \\+ baseUrl or cloudId \\(if one already exists\\).\n\nAlternatively, can we traverse the relationship in the other direction, i.e. can we request the tenant information from the edge using the provided cloudId, and then query the DB for the resulting baseUrl?",
    "markup": "markdown",
    "html": "<p>This could have woeful performance due to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>DB loading all of the client infos into memory from disk, sending across the network and loading into the application’s memory. Has the potential to OOM.</li>\n<li>Reaching out to the edge for potentially every client info if no match is found</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It would be much more preferable to push the filtering down to the DB itself, where possible, and only query for a matching product + baseUrl or cloudId (if one already exists).</p>\n<p>Alternatively, can we traverse the relationship in the other direction, i.e. can we request the tenant information from the edge using the provided cloudId, and then query the DB for the resulting baseUrl?</p>"
   },
